# US-China 2018 tariffs, reported only (ADR 0257 tier-2 free case). Rewritten 2026-11-06 for the
# C7 rerun: the file of the 28 October run was never committed and cannot be recovered.
#
# Source: Fajgelbaum, Goldberg, Kennedy and Khandelwal (2020), "The Return to Protectionism", QJE
# 135(1), Table I (NBER WP 25638). The model has two tariff groups (primary, manufactured) and no
# sector value weights are published for the lists, so each side's increase is the same on both
# groups: the increase on targeted trade times the share of the partner's trade targeted.
#
# US on CHN: lists China 1-3 (Table I panel A), targeted $247B of 2017 imports from China (48.8%
# of them, text of section II.B). Increase per list in points: 26.2-1.3 = 24.9 on $33.5B,
# 27.0-2.7 = 24.3 on $14.1B, 12.9-3.3 = 9.6 on $199.3B; trade-weighted 12.5 points on targeted
# trade; x 0.488 = 6.1 points on all imports from China. (Steel, aluminium, solar and washers hit
# other countries too and are left out.)
# CHN on USA: Table I panel B, China row: $92.5B of 2017 US exports, tariff 8.4% -> 18.9%, so
# +10.5 points on targeted trade; US goods exports to China in 2017 were about $127B (US-China
# Business Council, from Census data), so 92.5 / 127 = 0.73 targeted; 10.5 x 0.73 = 7.6 points.
tariff USA CHN 0 0.061
tariff USA CHN 1 0.061
tariff CHN USA 0 0.076
tariff CHN USA 1 0.076
